-- Q9 [Cell Types & Electron Flow · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans A] (g9)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Types & Electron Flow',
    $qt9$The decomposition of water, \(2\,\text{H}_2\text{O}(l) \rightarrow 2\,\text{H}_2(g) + \text{O}_2(g)\), has \(\Delta G^{\circ} = +474\ \text{kJ/mol}\) at \(298\ \text{K}\). To carry out this conversion, the reaction is run in a single cell in which a power supply is wired across two inert platinum electrodes, one labeled the anode and one labeled the cathode, with \(\text{H}_2\) collected at one electrode and \(\text{O}_2\) at the other. Taking \(F = 96{,}485\ \text{C/mol e}^-\), which set of statements correctly classifies this cell, gives the algebraic sign of its standard cell potential \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), and identifies the direction electrons travel through the external wire connecting the labeled electrodes?$qt9$,
    $op9$[{"label":"A","text":"Electrolytic cell; \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} \\approx -1.23\\ \\text{V}\\); electrons travel through the wire from the anode to the cathode."},{"label":"B","text":"Galvanic cell; \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} \\approx +1.23\\ \\text{V}\\); electrons travel through the wire from the anode to the cathode."},{"label":"C","text":"Electrolytic cell; \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} \\approx -1.23\\ \\text{V}\\); electrons travel through the wire from the cathode to the anode."},{"label":"D","text":"Electrolytic cell; \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} \\approx -2.46\\ \\text{V}\\); electrons travel through the wire from the anode to the cathode."}]$op9$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex9$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the positive free-energy change fixes the cell type, that cell type fixes the sign of the potential, and the definition of the electrodes fixes the electron direction, and all three must be made consistent at once. First, because \(\Delta G^{\circ} = +474\ \text{kJ/mol} > 0\), the reaction is non-spontaneous and cannot run on its own; it proceeds only when an external power supply forces it, which is exactly the definition of an electrolytic cell. Second, the sign of the potential follows from \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\). The overall change \(2\,\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow 2\,\text{H}_2 + \text{O}_2\) transfers \(n = 4\) electrons (each oxygen goes from \(-2\) to \(0\), losing \(2e^-\), for \(4e^-\) total per \(\text{O}_2\); equivalently four \(\text{H}^+\)-equivalents are reduced). Solving, \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = -\dfrac{\Delta G^{\circ}}{nF} = -\dfrac{+474{,}000\ \text{J/mol}}{(4)(96{,}485\ \text{C/mol})} = -1.23\ \text{V}\); the negative value is the hallmark of a driven, non-spontaneous (electrolytic) process. Third, the electron direction is fixed by the electrode definitions, which do not depend on cell type: oxidation always occurs at the anode and reduction always occurs at the cathode, so electrons are released at the anode and consumed at the cathode and therefore travel through the external wire from the anode to the cathode. (Choice B) misclassifies the cell as galvanic and reports \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} \approx +1.23\ \text{V}\); this reverses the meaning of the positive \(\Delta G^{\circ}\), which marks a non-spontaneous process (\(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} < 0\)), not a spontaneous one, even though its electron direction is stated correctly. (Choice C) keeps the correct cell type and potential but reverses the electron flow, claiming electrons move from cathode to anode; that is the direction of conventional current, not of electron motion, and electrons in fact always exit the anode and enter the cathode. (Choice D) uses the correct cell type and sign but computes the magnitude with \(n = 2\) instead of \(n = 4\), giving \(-474{,}000/[(2)(96{,}485)] = -2.46\ \text{V}\); this counts only two transferred electrons rather than the four required to release one \(\text{O}_2\). Skill 2$ex9$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc9$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc9$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a9B$Treats positive dG as spontaneous, labeling the driven cell galvanic with a positive E_cell, inverting the dG<->spontaneity relationship.$a9B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a9C$Confuses electron-flow direction with conventional-current direction, sending electrons cathode-to-anode instead of anode-to-cathode.$a9C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a9D$Uses n=2 instead of n=4 in dG=-nFE, doubling the potential magnitude to -2.46 V by undercounting transferred electrons.$a9D$ FROM q;

-- Q10 [Cell Types & Electron Flow · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans B] (g10)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Types & Electron Flow',
    $qt10$A galvanic concentration cell is constructed from two nickel electrodes, each dipping into an aqueous \(\text{Ni}^{2+}\) solution, with the two half-cells joined by a salt bridge. The half-cell on the left contains a dilute solution at \(0.010\ \text{M}\ \text{Ni}^{2+}\), while the half-cell on the right contains a concentrated solution at \(1.0\ \text{M}\ \text{Ni}^{2+}\). Because both electrodes are made of the same metal in contact with the same ion, the standard cell potential is \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = 0\ \text{V}\), and the cell operates spontaneously to reduce the concentration difference between the two compartments. As the cell discharges, which electrode is consumed (oxidized), in which direction do electrons travel through the external wire, and is the process spontaneous?$qt10$,
    $op10$[{"label":"A","text":"The concentrated (\\(1.0\\ \\text{M}\\)) electrode is oxidized; electrons flow through the wire from the concentrated side to the dilute side; the process is spontaneous."},{"label":"B","text":"The dilute (\\(0.010\\ \\text{M}\\)) electrode is oxidized; electrons flow through the wire from the dilute side to the concentrated side; the process is spontaneous."},{"label":"C","text":"The dilute (\\(0.010\\ \\text{M}\\)) electrode is oxidized; electrons flow through the wire from the concentrated side to the dilute side; the process is non-spontaneous."},{"label":"D","text":"The concentrated (\\(1.0\\ \\text{M}\\)) electrode is oxidized; electrons flow through the wire from the dilute side to the concentrated side; the process is spontaneous."}]$op10$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex10$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because a concentration cell runs in whatever direction drives the two compartments toward a single equalized concentration, and that requirement fixes both which electrode is the anode and the direction of electron flow. To shrink the gap between \(0.010\ \text{M}\) and \(1.0\ \text{M}\), the dilute side must gain dissolved \(\text{Ni}^{2+}\) while the concentrated side must lose it. The dilute electrode therefore undergoes oxidation, \(\text{Ni}(s) \rightarrow \text{Ni}^{2+} + 2e^-\), raising \([\text{Ni}^{2+}]\) on the dilute side; this electrode is the anode and is the one consumed. The concentrated electrode undergoes reduction, \(\text{Ni}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Ni}(s)\), lowering \([\text{Ni}^{2+}]\) on the concentrated side; it is the cathode. Because electrons always travel from anode to cathode through the external wire, they flow from the dilute side to the concentrated side. Spontaneity is confirmed by the Nernst equation with the net process \(\text{Ni}^{2+}(\text{conc}, 1.0\ \text{M}) \rightarrow \text{Ni}^{2+}(\text{dilute}, 0.010\ \text{M})\): \(Q = \frac{[\text{Ni}^{2+}]_{\text{dilute}}}{[\text{Ni}^{2+}]_{\text{conc}}} = \frac{0.010}{1.0} = 0.010\), so \(E_{\text{cell}} = 0 - \frac{0.0592}{2}\log(0.010) = -\,(0.0296)(-2) = +0.0592\ \text{V}\). Since \(E_{\text{cell}} > 0\), \(\Delta G = -nFE_{\text{cell}} < 0\) and the process is spontaneous. (Choice A) This reverses the cell direction: it claims the concentrated electrode is oxidized, which would make the concentrated side even more concentrated and the dilute side even more dilute, widening the gradient rather than closing it. That direction has \(E_{\text{cell}} = -0.0592\ \text{V} < 0\) and is the non-spontaneous reverse reaction, so labeling it spontaneous is incorrect. (Choice C) This correctly identifies the dilute electrode as the one oxidized but then states electrons flow from the concentrated (cathode) side to the dilute (anode) side, which inverts the rule that electrons exit the anode and enter the cathode; it also mislabels the process as non-spontaneous despite \(E_{\text{cell}} = +0.0592\ \text{V} > 0\). (Choice D) This pairs the wrong oxidized electrode (concentrated) with the correct anode-to-cathode electron direction (dilute to concentrated); these two statements are mutually inconsistent, because the electrode that is oxidized is by definition the anode from which electrons depart. Skill 2$ex10$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc10$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc10$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a10A$flips which side oxidizes so the gradient widens, calling the non-spontaneous reverse direction spontaneous$a10A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a10C$correct anode but reverses electron-flow direction and mislabels spontaneity$a10C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a10D$pairs the concentrated electrode as oxidized with an anode-to-cathode direction that contradicts it$a10D$ FROM q;

-- Q11 [Cell Types & Electron Flow · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans C] (g11)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Types & Electron Flow',
    $qt11$A single redox pairing can be operated in two opposite ways. Using the standard reduction potentials \(\text{Ag}^{+} + e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Ag},\ E^{\circ} = +0.80\ \text{V}\) and \(\text{Cu}^{2+} + 2e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Cu},\ E^{\circ} = +0.34\ \text{V}\), a chemist first lets the copper/silver couple run on its own as a spontaneous galvanic cell, then connects an external power supply to force the very same overall chemistry to proceed in the reverse direction in an electrolytic cell. Which choice most likely describes, correctly and completely, how the copper electrode's role, the direction of electron flow through the external wire, and the sign of \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) compare between the spontaneous galvanic operation and the forced electrolytic reversal?$qt11$,
    $op11$[{"label":"A","text":"Silver is the anode in the galvanic cell, with electrons flowing silver-to-copper at \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} = +0.46\\ \\text{V}\\); forcing the reverse makes silver the cathode and reverses the electron flow to copper-to-silver at \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} = -0.46\\ \\text{V}\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"Copper is the anode in both modes, so electrons flow copper-to-silver in both; \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} = +0.46\\ \\text{V}\\) in the galvanic cell and \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} = -0.46\\ \\text{V}\\) in the electrolytic cell."},{"label":"C","text":"Copper is the anode in the galvanic cell, with electrons flowing copper-to-silver at \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} = +0.46\\ \\text{V}\\); forcing the reverse makes copper the cathode and reverses the electron flow to silver-to-copper at \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} = -0.46\\ \\text{V}\\)."},{"label":"D","text":"Copper is the anode in the galvanic cell and becomes the cathode when reversed, and the electron flow reverses from copper-to-silver to silver-to-copper; but because it is the same chemistry, \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} = +0.46\\ \\text{V}\\) in both modes."}]$op11$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex11$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because every electrode identity, the wire electron direction, and the sign of \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) must each be tracked separately for the two modes, and all three flip together when the cell is forced backward. In the spontaneous galvanic cell the species with the higher standard reduction potential is reduced: \(\text{Ag}^{+}\) (\(+0.80\ \text{V}\)) is reduced at the cathode and copper metal is oxidized at the anode, giving the spontaneous reaction \(\text{Cu} + 2\,\text{Ag}^{+} \rightarrow \text{Cu}^{2+} + 2\,\text{Ag}\). Copper is therefore the anode (the negative terminal of a galvanic cell), and electrons always travel from the anode to the cathode through the external wire, i.e. copper-to-silver. The cell potential is \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = 0.80 - 0.34 = +0.46\ \text{V}\), which is positive, consistent with a spontaneous galvanic process (\(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} < 0\)). When an external supply forces the identical chemistry to run in reverse, \(\text{Cu}^{2+} + 2\,\text{Ag} \rightarrow \text{Cu} + 2\,\text{Ag}^{+}\), the half-reaction roles swap: copper ion is now reduced, so the copper electrode becomes the cathode, and silver is now oxidized at the anode. Because electrons still exit the anode and enter the cathode, the wire direction reverses to silver-to-copper. The driven direction is the non-spontaneous one, so its standard cell potential is the negative of the galvanic value: \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = 0.34 - 0.80 = -0.46\ \text{V}\), the hallmark of an electrolytic cell (\(\Delta G^{\circ} > 0\), requiring external work). All three features—copper switching anode→cathode, electron flow reversing copper-to-silver→silver-to-copper, and \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) going \(+0.46\ \text{V} \rightarrow -0.46\ \text{V}\)—match only choice C. (Choice A) This reverses which metal is oxidized in the galvanic cell, naming silver as the galvanic anode; that error stems from treating the higher reduction potential of \(\text{Ag}^{+}\) as a tendency to be oxidized, when in fact the higher \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\) means \(\text{Ag}^{+}\) is preferentially reduced and copper is the species oxidized at the anode. (Choice B) This holds copper fixed as the anode in both modes, so it keeps the electron flow as copper-to-silver in both; it correctly negates \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) but fails to recognize that forcing the reverse reaction swaps every electrode's role, making copper the cathode and reversing the wire direction. (Choice D) This correctly swaps copper to the cathode and reverses the electron flow but keeps \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) positive in both modes; it overlooks that driving the non-spontaneous reverse direction inverts the sign of the cell potential to \(-0.46\ \text{V}\), even though the chemistry involves the same couple. Skill 2$ex11$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc11$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc11$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a11A$Treats the higher reduction potential of Ag+ as a tendency to be oxidized, naming silver (not copper) as the galvanic anode and inverting both electrode roles and electron direction throughout.$a11A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a11B$Holds copper fixed as the anode in both modes, failing to swap electrode roles when the reaction is forced in reverse, so electron flow is left unchanged even though E_cell is correctly negated.$a11B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a11D$Correctly swaps electrode roles and electron direction but keeps E_cell positive in both modes, missing that driving the non-spontaneous reverse direction inverts the sign of the cell potential.$a11D$ FROM q;

-- Q12 [Electrode Charge Designations · easy · Skill 1 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans D] (g12)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrode Charge Designations',
    $qt12$In a Daniell galvanic (voltaic) cell, a zinc electrode is immersed in aqueous \(\text{Zn}^{2+}\) and a copper electrode is immersed in aqueous \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\), with the two half-cells joined by a salt bridge. As the cell discharges spontaneously, zinc is oxidized at one electrode while \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) is reduced at the other. What is the electrical charge sign assigned to the zinc electrode in this operating cell?$qt12$,
    $op12$[{"label":"A","text":"Positive, because it is the cathode of a galvanic cell"},{"label":"B","text":"Positive, because it is the anode of a galvanic cell"},{"label":"C","text":"Negative, because it is the cathode of a galvanic cell"},{"label":"D","text":"Negative, because it is the anode of a galvanic cell"}]$op12$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex12$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because zinc is the metal being oxidized (\(\text{Zn} \rightarrow \text{Zn}^{2+} + 2e^-\)), so by definition the zinc electrode is the anode. In a galvanic (voltaic) cell the reaction is spontaneous (\(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}>0\), \(\Delta G^{\circ}<0\)), and electrons are released by the oxidation reaction and flow out from the anode through the external wire toward the cathode. Because the anode is the source of these electrons, it carries an excess of negative charge and is therefore designated the negative (\(-\)) electrode in a galvanic cell. Thus the zinc electrode is the negative anode. (Choice A) This choice is wrong on both counts, labeling the oxidation electrode as a positive cathode, which inverts both the electrode role and the galvanic-cell sign convention. (Choice B) This choice correctly identifies zinc as the anode but assigns it a positive sign; the positive anode designation belongs to electrolytic cells, not galvanic cells, where the anode is negative. (Choice C) This choice gives the correct sign (negative) but misidentifies the electrode as the cathode; the cathode is where reduction occurs (the copper electrode), not where zinc is oxidized. Skill 1$ex12$,
    'easy', '4C', $cc12$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc12$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a12A$Inverts both electrode role and charge sign$a12A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a12B$Confuses galvanic anode sign with electrolytic anode (anode positive)$a12B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a12C$Right sign but swaps anode and cathode roles$a12C$ FROM q;

-- Q13 [Electrode Charge Designations · medium · Skill 1 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans B] (g14)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrode Charge Designations',
    $qt14$A silver spoon is electroplated by making it one electrode in an electrolytic cell containing aqueous silver nitrate, \(\text{AgNO}_3\). The cell is driven by an external battery, and at the spoon the half-reaction \(\text{Ag}^{+} + e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Ag}(s)\) deposits a metallic silver coating. To which terminal of the battery is the spoon connected, and what is the sign of this electrode?$qt14$,
    $op14$[{"label":"A","text":"The positive terminal; the spoon is the positive electrode"},{"label":"B","text":"The negative terminal; the spoon is the negative electrode"},{"label":"C","text":"The negative terminal; the spoon is the positive electrode"},{"label":"D","text":"The positive terminal; the spoon is the negative electrode"}]$op14$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex14$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the half-reaction at the spoon, \(\text{Ag}^{+} + e^{-} \rightarrow \text{Ag}(s)\), is a gain of electrons, i.e., reduction, and reduction always defines the cathode; so the spoon is the cathode in both cell types. In an electrolytic cell an external power source drives a non-spontaneous reaction by pushing electrons out of its negative terminal and into the electrode where reduction must occur. The battery therefore supplies the electrons consumed by this reduction from its negative terminal, so the spoon (cathode) must be wired to the battery's negative terminal and is itself the negative electrode. This is the defining sign convention for electrolytic cells: the cathode is negative and the anode is positive, which is the reverse of a galvanic cell. (Choice A) This gives the galvanic-cell convention, in which the cathode is the positive electrode; it incorrectly applies the spontaneous-cell sign assignment to a battery-driven electrolytic cell. (Choice C) Wiring to the negative terminal is correct, but labeling that cathode as positive contradicts itself: an electrode connected to the negative terminal of the source is the negative electrode. (Choice D) This pairs the positive terminal with a negative sign, an impossible electrolytic assignment, since an electrode wired to the positive terminal is the positive anode, not a negative cathode. Skill 1$ex14$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc14$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc14$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a14A$applies the galvanic-cell convention (cathode positive) to an electrolytic cell$a14A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a14C$correct negative terminal but self-contradictory positive sign label$a14C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a14D$pairs the positive terminal with a negative cathode sign, an impossible electrolytic assignment$a14D$ FROM q;

-- Q14 [Electrode Charge Designations · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans C] (g15)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrode Charge Designations',
    $qt15$In a Downs-type cell, molten \(\text{NaCl}\) is electrolyzed by an external power source to produce sodium metal and chlorine gas. As the cell runs, the \(\text{Cl}^-\) ions in the melt migrate toward one of the electrodes. Toward which electrode do the \(\text{Cl}^-\) ions migrate, what is the sign of that electrode in this cell, and what process occurs there?$qt15$,
    $op15$[{"label":"A","text":"The anode, which is the negative electrode, where \\(\\text{Cl}^-\\) is oxidized to \\(\\text{Cl}_2\\) gas"},{"label":"B","text":"The cathode, which is the positive electrode, where \\(\\text{Cl}^-\\) is reduced to \\(\\text{Cl}_2\\) gas"},{"label":"C","text":"The anode, which is the positive electrode, where \\(\\text{Cl}^-\\) is oxidized to \\(\\text{Cl}_2\\) gas"},{"label":"D","text":"The anode, which is the positive electrode, where \\(\\text{Cl}^-\\) is reduced to \\(\\text{Cl}_2\\) gas"}]$op15$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex15$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because three facts must line up. First, \(\text{Cl}^-\) is an anion, and in every electrochemical cell the anode attracts anions while the cathode attracts cations; therefore \(\text{Cl}^-\) migrates toward the anode. Second, the anode is by definition the oxidation site (An Ox: Anode = Oxidation), and there \(\text{Cl}^-\) loses electrons: \(2\,\text{Cl}^- \rightarrow \text{Cl}_2(g) + 2e^-\), producing chlorine gas. Third, the melting of \(\text{NaCl}\) electrolysis is driven by an external power source against a non-spontaneous reaction (\(\Delta G > 0\), \(E_{\text{cell}} < 0\)), so this is an electrolytic cell; in an electrolytic cell the anode is connected to the positive terminal of the source, making the anode the positive electrode. Anode + positive + oxidation of \(\text{Cl}^-\) to \(\text{Cl}_2\) all describe choice C. (Choice A) This correctly identifies the anode and the oxidation of \(\text{Cl}^-\) to \(\text{Cl}_2\), but it assigns the galvanic-cell polarity (anode negative) to an electrolytic cell; in an electrolytic cell the polarity is reversed, so the anode is positive, not negative. (Choice B) This places \(\text{Cl}^-\) at the cathode and calls the process reduction; it reverses the migration rule (anions go to the anode, not the cathode) and mislabels the half-reaction, since reduction is a gain of electrons whereas converting \(\text{Cl}^-\) to \(\text{Cl}_2\) is a loss of electrons (oxidation). (Choice D) This correctly identifies the anode as the positive electrode but calls the process reduction; the anode is always the oxidation site, and \(2\,\text{Cl}^- \rightarrow \text{Cl}_2 + 2e^-\) is an oxidation, not a reduction. Skill 2$ex15$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc15$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc15$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
