-- Q17 [Electrode Charge Designations · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans C] (g19)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrode Charge Designations',
    $qt19$A chemist takes a single redox couple and runs it in two different modes using a pair of generic metal electrodes. First the couple is allowed to react on its own as a spontaneous galvanic cell. Then an external power supply is wired across the same two electrodes so that the identical couple is now operated as a forced (electrolytic) cell. The chemist focuses specifically on the electrode that functions as the anode and asks how two of its features compare between the two modes: (i) the chemical role of that electrode, defined as whether oxidation or reduction takes place at it, and (ii) the algebraic charge sign (\(+\) or \(-\)) assigned to that electrode. Which statement correctly describes how the anode's chemical role and its charge sign compare across the galvanic and electrolytic modes?$qt19$,
    $op19$[{"label":"A","text":"The chemical role flips and the charge sign flips: the electrode that is the anode in the galvanic mode becomes the cathode in the electrolytic mode, and its charge sign therefore changes from \\(-\\) to \\(+\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"Both features are invariant: the anode remains the oxidation site and keeps the same charge sign in both modes, because the labels \"anode\" and \"cathode\" are fixed to a given electrode once a cell is built."},{"label":"C","text":"The chemical role is invariant but the charge sign flips: the anode is the oxidation site in both modes, yet its charge sign changes from \\(-\\) in the galvanic mode to \\(+\\) in the electrolytic mode."},{"label":"D","text":"The chemical role is invariant and the charge sign is invariant, but the direction of electron flow through the external wire reverses: electrons leave the anode in the galvanic mode but enter the anode in the electrolytic mode."}]$op19$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex19$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the term anode is defined by chemistry, not by charge: the anode is the electrode where oxidation occurs, and that definition holds in every electrochemical cell, galvanic or electrolytic. So the chemical role of the anode is invariant across the two modes. What changes is the charge sign. In a spontaneous galvanic cell the anode is the source of electrons being pushed out by the spontaneous reaction, so it carries a negative (\(-\)) sign. In a forced electrolytic cell, the external power supply pulls electrons out of the anode by attaching it to the positive terminal, so the anode now carries a positive (\(+\)) sign. Thus the role stays the same (oxidation) while the sign flips from \(-\) (galvanic) to \(+\) (electrolytic), exactly as stated in C. (Choice A) This reverses the role: it claims the galvanic anode becomes the electrolytic cathode. The anode is fixed by the location of oxidation, so an electrode that is the oxidation site remains the anode in both modes; the role does not switch even though the sign does. (Choice B) This treats both features as invariant by claiming the anode keeps the same charge sign. The labels anode and cathode are tied to oxidation and reduction, not to a permanent charge, and the charge sign of the anode does flip (\(-\) galvanic, \(+\) electrolytic) when the cell is forced. (Choice D) This holds the charge sign fixed and instead reverses the electron-flow direction. By convention electrons always travel from anode to cathode through the external wire in both cell types, so electrons leave the anode in both modes; the electron-flow direction relative to the anode does not reverse, whereas the anode's charge sign does. Skill 2$ex19$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc19$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc19$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a19A$swaps the role-invariant anode into a cathode, conflating charge-sign reversal with electrode-label reversal$a19A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a19B$treats electrode charge sign as permanently fixed to an electrode, so nothing flips between cell types$a19B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a19D$holds the charge sign constant and instead invents a reversal of external-wire electron-flow direction$a19D$ FROM q;

-- Q18 [Electrode Charge Designations · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans D] (g20)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Electrode Charge Designations',
    $qt20$An unlabeled two-electrode cell is connected by an external wire, and a chemist makes only two observations. First, the conventional current in the external wire is directed from electrode X to electrode Y. Second, dissolved \(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\) anions within the cell drift toward electrode Y. The chemist has no information about whether the cell is spontaneous or driven by an external power source, and no voltmeter or potential values are available. Based strictly on these two observations, which choice most accurately designates electrodes X and Y?$qt20$,
    $op20$[{"label":"A","text":"X is the cathode (the site of reduction) and Y is the anode (the site of oxidation); because the anode is the positive (\\(+\\)) electrode, Y is the (\\(+\\)) electrode and X is the (\\(-\\)) electrode."},{"label":"B","text":"X is the anode (the site of oxidation) and Y is the cathode (the site of reduction); the charge signs of X and Y cannot be determined from these observations alone."},{"label":"C","text":"X is the cathode (the site of reduction) and Y is the anode (the site of oxidation); the cell must be galvanic, so X is the positive (\\(+\\)) electrode and Y is the negative (\\(-\\)) electrode."},{"label":"D","text":"X is the cathode (the site of reduction) and Y is the anode (the site of oxidation); however, because the cell could be either galvanic or electrolytic, the \\(+\\) and \\(-\\) charge signs of X and Y cannot be determined from these observations alone."}]$op20$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex20$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the two observations together fix the oxidation/reduction roles of both electrodes but, critically, do NOT reveal the cell type, so the charge signs remain indeterminate. Work the current clue first: in the external wire, electrons always travel from the anode to the cathode, so conventional current (the flow of positive charge, opposite to electron flow) always travels from the cathode to the anode through the wire. Current directed from X to Y therefore makes X the source of conventional current in the wire, i.e. the cathode, and Y the destination, i.e. the anode. The anion clue is an independent cross-check: in every electrochemical cell the anode attracts anions (and the cathode attracts cations), so \(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\) drifting toward Y confirms that Y is the anode. Thus X is the cathode (reduction) and Y is the anode (oxidation), and these roles are absolute — oxidation occurs at the anode and reduction at the cathode in both galvanic and electrolytic cells. The controlling twist is that neither datum distinguishes a galvanic cell from an electrolytic one: the external-wire current runs cathode-to-anode and the anions migrate to the anode in BOTH cell types. Because the cell type is unknown, the polarity is unknown too — in a galvanic cell the cathode is \(+\) and the anode is \(-\), whereas in an electrolytic cell the cathode is \(-\) and the anode is \(+\) — so the \(+/-\) signs of X and Y cannot be assigned from these observations alone. (Choice A) correctly identifies the roles but invokes the false universal rule that "the anode is the positive electrode"; the anode is positive only in an electrolytic cell, while in a galvanic cell the anode is negative, so this anode-sign-flip reasoning assigns signs that cannot be justified without first establishing the cell type. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must translate current direction into an electrode identity, cross-check it against anion migration, and then recognize that neither observation is a cell-type discriminator, so the redox roles are fixed while the charge signs remain indeterminate. (Choice B) reverses the roles, making X the anode and Y the cathode; this requires reading the wire current as flowing cathode-to-anode in the direction Y-to-X (or believing anions migrate to the cathode), both of which invert the actual conventions, since current in the wire runs cathode-to-anode and anions go to the anode. (Choice C) correctly identifies X as the cathode and Y as the anode but over-classifies the cell as galvanic and then applies the galvanic sign convention (anode \(-\), cathode \(+\)); the observations are equally consistent with an electrolytic cell, so the galvanic assumption — and the signs that follow from it — is unjustified. Skill 2$ex20$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc20$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc20$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a20A$The anode-sign-flip trap: applies the electrolytic-only rule 'the anode is the positive electrode' as if universal, making the anode Y positive and cathode X negative without first determining the cell type.$a20A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a20B$Inverts both conventions at once, reading external-wire current as anode-to-cathode and anion migration toward the cathode, so X is called the anode and Y the cathode.$a20B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a20C$Gets the cathode/anode roles right but over-classifies the cell as galvanic and then assigns galvanic polarity (cathode +, anode -), even though the data fit an electrolytic cell equally well.$a20C$ FROM q;

-- Q19 [Cell Diagram Notation · easy · Skill 1 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans A] (g21)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Diagram Notation',
    $qt21$A galvanic cell is constructed as follows: a nickel electrode is immersed in a 1 M \(\text{Ni}^{2+}\) solution and serves as the anode, while a silver electrode is immersed in a 1 M \(\text{Ag}^{+}\) solution and serves as the cathode. The two half-cells are connected by a salt bridge. Which of the following correctly represents this cell using standard shorthand line notation?$qt21$,
    $op21$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Ni(s)}\\mid\\text{Ni}^{2+}(1\\ \\text{M})\\parallel\\text{Ag}^{+}(1\\ \\text{M})\\mid\\text{Ag(s)}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{Ag(s)}\\mid\\text{Ag}^{+}(1\\ \\text{M})\\parallel\\text{Ni}^{2+}(1\\ \\text{M})\\mid\\text{Ni(s)}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Ni(s)}\\parallel\\text{Ni}^{2+}(1\\ \\text{M})\\mid\\text{Ag}^{+}(1\\ \\text{M})\\parallel\\text{Ag(s)}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Ni}^{2+}(1\\ \\text{M})\\mid\\text{Ni(s)}\\parallel\\text{Ag(s)}\\mid\\text{Ag}^{+}(1\\ \\text{M})\\)"}]$op21$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex21$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because standard cell (line) notation always writes the anode half-cell on the left and the cathode half-cell on the right. Here nickel is the anode, so the left side begins with the electrode metal and proceeds to its ion: \(\text{Ni(s)}\mid\text{Ni}^{2+}(1\ \text{M})\). A single vertical bar \(\mid\) marks a phase boundary within a half-cell (solid metal in contact with aqueous ion). The double vertical bar \(\parallel\) represents the salt bridge separating the two half-cells. Silver is the cathode, so the right side proceeds from its ion to the electrode metal: \(\text{Ag}^{+}(1\ \text{M})\mid\text{Ag(s)}\). Assembled, the cell is \(\text{Ni(s)}\mid\text{Ni}^{2+}(1\ \text{M})\parallel\text{Ag}^{+}(1\ \text{M})\mid\text{Ag(s)}\), which is choice A. (Choice B) reverses the convention by placing the silver (cathode) half-cell on the left and the nickel (anode) half-cell on the right; line notation requires the anode on the left and the cathode on the right, so the entire diagram is flipped. (Choice C) uses the wrong separators: it places the double bar \(\parallel\) (which denotes the salt bridge) between an electrode and its own ion and uses a single bar \(\mid\) where the salt bridge belongs, inverting the meaning of \(\mid\) and \(\parallel\). (Choice D) writes each half-cell in the wrong internal order, listing the aqueous ion before its electrode metal on the anode side and the metal before its ion on the cathode side, rather than reading metal-to-ion on the left and ion-to-metal on the right. Skill 1$ex21$,
    'easy', '4C', $cc21$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc21$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a21B$Anode and cathode half-cells placed on the wrong sides (whole diagram flipped)$a21B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a21C$Single bar and double bar swapped (salt-bridge symbol used for phase boundary and vice versa)$a21C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a21D$Incorrect within-half-cell ordering (ion before metal on anode side)$a21D$ FROM q;

-- Q20 [Cell Diagram Notation · easy · Skill 1 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans B] (g22)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Diagram Notation',
    $qt22$A student is given the following galvanic cell diagram and is told it uses standard cell-diagram (line) notation: \(\text{Pb}(s)\,|\,\text{Pb}^{2+}(aq)\,||\,\text{Fe}^{3+}(aq),\ \text{Fe}^{2+}(aq)\,|\,\text{Pt}(s)\) Based on the conventions of cell-diagram notation, which species is oxidized at the anode of this cell?$qt22$,
    $op22$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Pb}^{2+}(aq)\\), at the left-hand electrode"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{Pb}(s)\\), at the left-hand electrode"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Fe}^{3+}(aq)\\), at the right-hand electrode"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Pt}(s)\\), at the right-hand electrode"}]$op22$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex22$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. In cell-diagram (line) notation, the anode (the site of oxidation) is always written on the left, the cathode (the site of reduction) is written on the right, a single vertical bar \(|\) marks a phase boundary, and the double bar \(||\) marks the salt bridge separating the two half-cells. The answer to this question is B because the left-hand half-cell, \(\text{Pb}(s)\,|\,\text{Pb}^{2+}(aq)\), is the anode by convention, and the species written as the reactant entering that half-reaction from the electrode is the solid metal \(\text{Pb}(s)\). Solid lead loses electrons and is oxidized: \(\text{Pb}(s) \rightarrow \text{Pb}^{2+}(aq) + 2e^-\). The lead metal itself serves as the left-hand electrode, so \(\text{Pb}(s)\) is the species oxidized at the anode. (Choice A) \(\text{Pb}^{2+}(aq)\) is the product of the anode half-reaction, not the species oxidized; it is what \(\text{Pb}(s)\) becomes after losing electrons, so naming the ion confuses the product of oxidation with the reactant being oxidized. (Choice C) \(\text{Fe}^{3+}(aq)\) is on the right (cathode) side, where reduction occurs (\(\text{Fe}^{3+} + e^- \rightarrow \text{Fe}^{2+}\)); it gains an electron rather than losing one, so it is reduced, not oxidized, and it sits at the cathode, not the anode. (Choice D) \(\text{Pt}(s)\) is an inert electrode that merely provides a conducting surface for the \(\text{Fe}^{3+}/\text{Fe}^{2+}\) couple on the right; it is the cathode material and does not itself undergo oxidation, so identifying it as oxidized reverses the left-anode/right-cathode convention. Skill 1$ex22$,
    'easy', '4C', $cc22$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc22$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a22A$names the dissolved ion (product of oxidation) instead of the solid metal reactant being oxidized$a22A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a22C$treats the right-side cathode species as oxidized, confusing reduction (Fe3+ to Fe2+) with oxidation$a22C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a22D$places oxidation at the right-hand electrode, reversing the left-anode/right-cathode convention$a22D$ FROM q;

-- Q21 [Cell Diagram Notation · medium · Skill 1 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans C] (g23)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Diagram Notation',
    $qt23$A galvanic cell is represented by the cell diagram \(\text{Cr(s)}\,|\,\text{Cr}^{3+}\text{(aq)}\,||\,\text{Sn}^{2+}\text{(aq)}\,|\,\text{Sn(s)}\). The relevant standard reduction potentials are \(E^\circ(\text{Sn}^{2+}/\text{Sn}) = -0.14\ \text{V}\) and \(E^\circ(\text{Cr}^{3+}/\text{Cr}) = -0.74\ \text{V}\). Reading the diagram and confirming that the left half-cell is the anode is consistent with these potentials, what does the double vertical line \(||\) in this diagram represent?$qt23$,
    $op23$[{"label":"A","text":"The external wire through which electrons travel from one electrode to the other."},{"label":"B","text":"A phase boundary between a solid electrode and the aqueous solution that contacts it."},{"label":"C","text":"The salt bridge, which connects and separates the two half-cells while allowing ion flow between them."},{"label":"D","text":"The location of the anode, where oxidation occurs in the cell."}]$op23$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex23$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because, by convention, a cell diagram is written as anode | anode solution || cathode solution | cathode, so the double vertical line \(||\) denotes the salt bridge that physically separates the two half-cells while permitting the migration of ions needed to maintain charge balance. The given potentials confirm the orientation: with \(\text{Cr}\) as the left (anode) electrode and \(\text{Sn}\) as the right (cathode) electrode, \(E^\circ_{cell} = E^\circ_{red,cathode} - E^\circ_{red,anode} = (-0.14\ \text{V}) - (-0.74\ \text{V}) = +0.60\ \text{V}\), which is positive, so the cell is spontaneous (galvanic) exactly as written. In \(\text{Cr(s)}\,|\,\text{Cr}^{3+}\text{(aq)}\,||\,\text{Sn}^{2+}\text{(aq)}\,|\,\text{Sn(s)}\), the \(||\) sits between the \(\text{Cr}^{3+}\) (anode) half-cell on the left and the \(\text{Sn}^{2+}\) (cathode) half-cell on the right, exactly where the salt bridge belongs; the single vertical lines \(|\), by contrast, mark phase boundaries within a single half-cell (e.g., between solid \(\text{Cr}\) and aqueous \(\text{Cr}^{3+}\)). (Choice A) The external wire carries electrons between electrodes, but it is not depicted by any symbol in standard cell-diagram notation; the conducting pathway for ions inside the cell (the salt bridge) is what \(||\) represents, not the electron wire. (Choice B) A phase boundary between a solid electrode and its aqueous solution is denoted by a single vertical line \(|\), not the double line \(||\); this choice assigns the meaning of \(|\) to the \(||\) symbol. (Choice D) The anode is identified by its position at the far left of the diagram (\(\text{Cr(s)}\)) and confirmed by the potentials, not by the double vertical line; \(||\) marks the salt bridge separating the half-cells rather than locating the electrode where oxidation occurs. Skill 1$ex23$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc23$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc23$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
